"<span>Blind: hide the totals, verdicts, identifications and computed momenta and masses</span>",
"Particles: the Standard Model chart and the four forces follow §{#} and Table {#} of the book; the groups (leptons, mesons, baryons; fermions with half integer spin, bosons with whole number spin) follow §{#} and Table {#}, and the conserved numbers Table {#}. Masses, mean lives and decays are from the Particle Data Group's {#} Review of Particle Physics. Quark masses are not measured on free quarks (none exist): they are PDG's running masses, which depend on the scheme. A mean life marked ħ/Γ is worked out from the measured width Γ of a very short lived particle.",
"Reactions: the checker adds up charge, baryon number, the three lepton numbers and strangeness on each side (§{#}), counts particles of half integer spin (angular momentum can change only by whole units of ħ, so that count must change by an even number), and compares the energy available with the rest energy of the products. For a decay the energy is the parent's rest energy; for a beam of kinetic energy K on a target at rest, W² = m₁² + m₂² + {#}(K + m₁)m₂ (c = {#}); for two head on beams of energy E each, W² = {#}(E² + p₁p₂) + m₁² + m₂² (Eq. {#}). Making a single particle needs W at its mass, within its width. A reaction that passes is put down to the weak interaction if a neutrino, W or Z takes part or a quark changes flavor (the weak interaction changes strangeness by at most one unit), to the electromagnetic interaction if a photon or a charged lepton takes part, and otherwise to the strong interaction. These rules cover the book's conservation laws only: a few processes that pass are still forbidden by other symmetries (π⁰ → {#}γ breaks charge conjugation) or are very rare.",
"Quarks: charges, baryon numbers and strangeness from Table {#}; quark content of hadrons from Tables {#} and {#} and PDG {#}. Only ground states are built (no orbital motion), so the spin is the sum of the quark spins. A hadron must be color neutral (§{#}): red, green and blue for a baryon, a color and its anticolor for a meson. Three quarks of one flavor can only have spin {#}/{#} in the ground state. The neutral mesons uū, dd̄ and ss̄ are mixtures, so one quark content can be several particles (π⁰, η, η′), and uds is either the Λ⁰ or the Σ⁰, which differ in how the u and d are combined. The eightfold way diagrams place each hadron by isospin I₃ = (number of u − number of d)/{#} and strangeness, with dashed lines of equal charge, Q = I₃ + (B + S)/{#}. The quarks moving in the picture are only a picture.",
"Detector (simulated data): a liquid hydrogen bubble chamber, window {#} m across, in a uniform field B into the page. A π⁻ beam meets protons at rest; one beam pion makes π⁻ + p → K⁰ + Λ⁰ where its track ends. The K⁰ is a K⁰_S half the time ({#}% of those decay to π⁺ π⁻, the rest to two π⁰, which leave no tracks) or a K⁰_L, which escapes; the Λ⁰ decays to p π⁻ {#}% of the time (PDG {#}). Flight distances follow the exponential decay law with the PDG mean lives, and the decays use exact two body relativistic kinematics. Approximations: all tracks lie in the plane of the picture (real chambers use stereo cameras to find the dip angle), the production and decay angles are chosen uniformly in that plane rather than over all directions, the particles lose no energy and do not scatter in the hydrogen (so tracks are exact circles; a real proton slows and spirals), tracks are drawn for at most one turn, bubble spacing goes roughly as β² (slow, heavy tracks are denser), and each bubble is scattered by the chosen amount about the true path. Only events with at least one V inside the window are shown, as a scanner would choose them; each event number always gives the same event. Your circle fit is a least squares (Kåsa) fit to the points you place, and p = {#} B r with p in MeV/c, B in T and r in cm (the book's Eq. {#}, p = {#} B r in GeV/c, T and m, rounds {#} to {#}). For comparison, LHC dipoles of {#} T bend {#} TeV protons on a radius r = p/({#} B) = {#} km.",
